
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When I first changed from Vim to Emacs a few years ago, the editor's user interface felt sluggish. I quickly learned that the Emacs UI itself is not slow, rather, its default settings can make it feel that way. To restore the instant feedback loop I was accustomed to, I needed to enhance Emacs defaults. (FYI: Enhancing default settings is what drove me to develop <a href="https://github.com/jamescherti/minimal-emacs.d">minimal-emacs.d</a>)</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The configurations below are the result of my tuning process to enhance the perceived Emacs UI responsiveness and latency. These settings are best treated as independent experiments. I recommend testing these adjustments iteratively.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Before we dive in, <strong>please consider sharing this article</strong> on your website/blog, Mastodon, Reddit, X, or your preferred social media platforms. Sharing it will help fellow Emacs users to enhance their configuration.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Show-paren delay</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>show-paren-delay</code> controls how long Emacs waits, after becoming idle, before highlighting a matching parenthesis when <code>show-paren-mode</code> is active:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> show-paren-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.05</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A shorter delay makes delimiter matching appear closer to the point at which the cursor reaches the parenthesis. This is pleasant in Lisp buffers, where matching delimiters carry structural information.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A very small value can make the highlight appear frequently while point is moving quickly through source code.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Fontification</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Font lock decoration level</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>font-lock-maximum-decoration</code> variable controls how much Font Lock highlighting major modes request.</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> font-lock-maximum-decoration '((<span class="hljs-literal">t</span> . <span class="hljs-number">2</span>)))</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Level 2 is a relatively minimal level of decoration. Higher levels generally add more syntax details. The tradeoff is reduced semantic information in the display. Functions, declarations, built-in names, and other constructs that appear at higher decoration levels may no longer receive their usual faces. (Reducing decoration lowers the amount of regular-expression and syntax analysis performed by modes.)</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For major modes that use Tree-sitter, use <code>treesit-font-lock-level</code>. Its default is 3. Level 1 usually fontifies comments and function names in function definitions. Level 2 additionally fontifies keywords, strings, and data types. Lowering the level can reduce the amount of fontification performed:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> treesit-font-lock-level <span class="hljs-number">2</span>)</code></span></pre>


<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Font cache compaction</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>inhibit-compacting-font-caches</code> variable prevents Emacs from compacting its font caches:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> inhibit-compacting-font-caches <span class="hljs-literal">t</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It can improve performance with large fonts by preventing font-cache compaction, at the cost of increased memory usage.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Skipping some fontification while input is pending</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>redisplay-skip-fontification-on-input</code> variable allows Emacs to skip some fontification when input is waiting:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> redisplay-skip-fontification-on-input <span class="hljs-literal">t</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">When input is pending, Emacs can skip fontification. This can make scrolling smoother by avoiding fontification work while Emacs is trying to keep up with incoming input.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Scrolling</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Scrolling can become slow when Emacs displays unfontified text. The following prevents Emacs from becoming unresponsive when you move rapidly through buffers:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> fast-but-imprecise-scrolling <span class="hljs-literal">t</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The main tradeoff is that scrolling can become visually imprecise. However, for typical coding tasks using a standard monospaced font, line heights remain consistent, meaning you will rarely experience this imprecision. Even in modes that do use variable font sizes, the visual jump is minor and temporary.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Which-key</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>which-key-idle-delay</code> variable controls how long <code>which-key-mode</code> waits after a prefix key is entered before displaying the available bindings:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> which-key-idle-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.5</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Reducing the delay makes key discovery appear sooner, but it also increases the likelihood that a popup will appear during an ordinary key sequence. (For experienced users with memorized prefixes, a longer delay can reduce visual interruptions.)</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Tooltip</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Tooltip delay</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>tooltip-delay</code> variable determines how long Emacs <code>tooltip-mode</code> waits before displaying the first tooltip after the pointer rests over an item that provides tooltip information:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> tooltip-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.3</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A shorter delay can also make accidental hover events more intrusive. (For keyboard-centric workflows, increasing the delay or disabling tooltips entirely may be preferable to making them appear more quickly.)</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Tooltip short delay</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>tooltip-short-delay</code> variable controls the delay before showing a subsequent tooltip after a tooltip has already been displayed:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> tooltip-short-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.08</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This setting is mainly about interaction latency rather than Emacs's general execution speed. Very short values can make mouse movement produce a succession of tooltip updates.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Third-party package: Evil mode</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>evil-ex-hl-update-delay</code> variable controls the amount of idle time before Evil updates search highlighting:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> evil-ex-hl-update-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.01</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lowering it to 0.01 seconds makes highlighting react a little sooner while editing an Ex search pattern. The variable does not make regular-expression searches faster. It reduces the delay before Evil starts the next highlight update.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Icomplete compute delay</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>icomplete-compute-delay</code> variable controls how long <code>icomplete</code> waits before computing completions after minibuffer input changes:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> icomplete-compute-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.01</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A value of 0.01 seconds makes icomplete react almost immediately, which reduces the grace period that allows another keystroke to arrive before computation begins.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Note: For large or computationally expensive completion tables, reducing this delay can instead cause more work to be performed during typing. The optimal value depends on the completion source.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Third-party package: Corfu</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>corfu-auto-delay</code> variable specifies how long Corfu waits before displaying automatic completion after its auto-completion conditions are met:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> corfu-auto-delay <span class="hljs-number">0.1</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lowering this delay speeds up when the completion popup appears, but it can introduce interface lag. The package documentation warns that aggressive values can increase the amount of work performed during typing. If completion computation is expensive, querying it more frequently can make typing less responsive.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Test different values incrementally until you find the right balance for your configuration.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Emacs on MS Windows</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For Emacs users on Windows, file I/O and subprocess communication are common sources of latency. Try the following settings:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">when</span> (<span class="hljs-name">eq</span> system-type 'windows-nt)
  (<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> w32-get-true-file-attributes <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>      <span class="hljs-comment">; Decrease file IO workload</span>
        w32-pipe-buffer-size (<span class="hljs-name">*</span> <span class="hljs-number">128</span> <span class="hljs-number">1024</span>)))   <span class="hljs-comment">; Read more at a time</span></code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This can reduce latency on Windows when working with language servers (LSP), asynchronous searches, and version control.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">PGTK frame event timeout</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <code>pgtk-wait-for-event-timeout</code> variable is for Emacs builds using the PGTK toolkit. It specifies how long Emacs may wait for graphical events after changes that affect graphical-interface state.</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">when</span> (<span class="hljs-name">boundp</span> 'pgtk-wait-for-event-timeout)
  (<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> pgtk-wait-for-event-timeout <span class="hljs-number">0.001</span>))</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Reducing the timeout reduces the maximum time Emacs waits for the relevant GTK event, which can reduce latency for operations that show or hide frames.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Input latency on Emacs GTK</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you experience keyboard input latency in Emacs GTK builds, it may be caused by the GTK input method interface. For users who do not rely on GTK input methods, the following UNIX/Linux environment variable can be tested:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> GTK_IM_MODULE=none</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This disables the GTK input method module for the Emacs process and can avoid latency caused by problematic GTK input methods.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Emacs can lag in its response to keyboard input when GTK input methods are used. Emacs receives keyboard input as unprocessed key events with metadata, unlike most GTK applications, and some modern input methods can misbehave in this configuration.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can also disable the input method context if key modifiers such as C-S-u are not reported correctly:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> pgtk-use-im-context <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>)</code></span></pre>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Window manager and frame operation delays</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Certain window managers fail to send expected confirmation events back to Emacs, which triggers pauses during interface adjustments.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If a frame operation causes a pause because the window manager does not confirm the requested geometry change, try preventing Emacs from waiting for the window manager:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp"><span class="hljs-comment">;; Apply to all future frames, including emacsclient frames</span>
(<span class="hljs-name">add-to-list</span> 'default-frame-alist '(wait-for-wm . nil))

<span class="hljs-comment">;; Apply immediately to the startup frame if a GUI frame exists</span>
(<span class="hljs-name">when</span> (<span class="hljs-name">display-graphic-p</span>)
  (<span class="hljs-name">modify-frame-parameters</span> <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span> '((wait-for-wm . <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>))))</code></span></pre>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Other causes of latency</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">X forwarded display</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If Emacs is slow on X forwarded displays, querying the pointer location can be the bottleneck. Try the following:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> mouse-highlight <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>)</code></span></pre>


<h3 class="wp-block-heading">The clipboard on X</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Emacs is slow to exit in X, it could be caused by clipboard timeouts. Emacs communicates with external clipboard managers during selection changes and session shutdown. If the external manager fails to respond promptly, Emacs blocks interactive execution while waiting for a response. Try the following:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> x-select-enable-clipboard-manager <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>)
(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> x-selection-timeout <span class="hljs-number">100</span>)</code></span></pre>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If selecting text with the mouse cause lag because external utilities poll selection, try:</p>


<pre class="wp-block-code"><span><code class="hljs language-lisp">(<span class="hljs-name">setq</span> select-active-regions <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>)</code></span></pre>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Conclusion</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Emacs responsiveness is influenced by several components, including redisplay, fontification, completion, and graphical event handling. The settings above target specific sources of perceived latency and are best evaluated individually.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">And you? Which settings have made the biggest difference in your setup? Which other settings do you recommend?</p>
